QuPath with DJL:basic exmaple with code
Integrating QuPath with DJL (Deep Java Library) allows you to run high-performance AI inference (PyTorch, TensorFlow, or ONNX) directly within a pathology workspace using Java/Groovy. 
In QuPath, you typically use a script to load a model and apply it to an image tile. 
🛠️ Basic QuPath + DJL Script
This Groovy script demonstrates how to initialize a DJL model and run a simple inference pass on the current image.
groovy
import ai.djl.repository.zoo.Criteria
import ai.djl.training.util.ProgressBar
import ai.djl.modality.cv.ImageFactory
import ai.djl.modality.cv.output.DetectedObjects
import ai.djl.repository.zoo.ZooModel

// 1. Define the Model Criteria (using a pre-trained model from DJL Zoo)
def criteria = Criteria.builder()
    .setTypes(Image.class, DetectedObjects.class)
    .optFilter("dataset", "coco")
    .optEngine("PyTorch") // You can also use "TensorFlow" or "OnnxRuntime"
    .build()

// 2. Load the model
try (ZooModel model = criteria.loadModel()) {
    def predictor = model.newPredictor()

    // 3. Get the current image from QuPath
    def viewer = getCurrentViewer()
    def imageData = getQuPath().getImageData()
    def server = imageData.getServer()
    
    // Get a 512x512 tile from the center
    int x = server.getWidth() / 2
    int y = server.getHeight() / 2
    def request = RegionRequest.createInstance(server.getPath(), 1.0, x, y, 512, 512)
    def img = ImageFactory.getInstance().fromImage(getCurrentImage())

    // 4. Run Inference
    def results = predictor.predict(img)
    println "Detection Results: " + results
}
Use code with caution.

🔑 Why Use DJL in QuPath?
Engine Agnostic: You can switch between PyTorch and TensorFlow without changing your Java code. Use the DJL Engine Selection guide to choose your backend.
Memory Management: DJL handles the "off-heap" memory management for GPUs, which is critical since QuPath runs on the JVM.
Integration: You can use the QuPath-Extension-DJL (available on GitHub) which provides a UI-friendly way to manage models without writing raw script every time. 
🚀 Getting Started
Install the Extension: Download and drag the QuPath DJL Extension JAR into your QuPath window.
Select Engine: Use the DJL Model Zoo to find pre-trained models for object detection or segmentation.
Run Scripts: Open the script editor (Automate -> Script Editor) and paste your Groovy code to interact with the DJL runtime. 
